_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/state.json" "file_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/state.json" "file_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d3a446f9562ec0de41761947788297a161d1086c70da4370254ebd7073a40dd"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/state.json" "file_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/state.json" "file_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "q-94054" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/state.json" "file_sha256" "3c713f835cfca54045907739e077837151d35744ed142660e7e682b56fb413e0"} {"question" "e-8157" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/c3a4052f80fc4a7202d410f3ea523e58507ac60399506c2abad9be164bce5251.png)\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/18abb9c5165a3fe54a0f3067cf5325bfd4bd0fa4243c841ce4e7bb95c25dddda.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8157.json" "file_sha256" "0bb3e2b0ddb96ae94c4e4fb03fecde025763317c1db25c053f79ee475ec3ac07"} {"question" "e-8157" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8157.json" "file_sha256" "0bb3e2b0ddb96ae94c4e4fb03fecde025763317c1db25c053f79ee475ec3ac07"} {"question" "e-8164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/9614ff63a66cf5428a4107828b08ba16c43e3e021f6620d408d8b70358a3e535.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/9acec021ce321e77ad541ecbb0a38944aa4991ec26c721e0e59a521490269d06.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8164.json" "file_sha256" "d7664f64705d0fac01f4231f494b615921c674ff8888bab8c9a52205be9bbd2f"} {"question" "e-8164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/efb691aec933aa631974add9dc9d7d122b03f5956b17459eb0e76751e07ea42a.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/8c0ac2201e3e40bcc8e5e71c12413504694b201237069a4695fae94d33e7fcb4.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/1490159efc1ac2073ee029f918c136b7c16fd77b5f15e59334a3ae63e08a8c92.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8164.json" "file_sha256" "d7664f64705d0fac01f4231f494b615921c674ff8888bab8c9a52205be9bbd2f"} {"question" "e-8158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/c344041bbcf1cc644f0cfb1e5a3ac8ecd687db483425e4f1f4bb19c76ebac439.png)\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8158.json" "file_sha256" "ad9b751e122378131ee791d931f278236c269d4ba62b52890ce0174602a7a2c5"} {"question" "e-8158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/7b0367066950407149521e6b06255f71b25fe5e67bc609fac5e914d3d1315fe7.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/498ecfed1cfe5ed4b1df8c02088045e67c4d5601e47f403288d271447c5ded28.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/0aa3aecabd941ccc8e176aef479eb3630197ff9c7fb6ca63e028e41315c9aafb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/1f494272dcd6828d437e2f566e46aa5b5ece49a92cd82af094e25834fe1bbcbe.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/737d19f3df39ef56518c98843cb7dd0359965570892b17df57fb4117bb0f5844.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8158.json" "file_sha256" "ad9b751e122378131ee791d931f278236c269d4ba62b52890ce0174602a7a2c5"} {"question" "q-94056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "a3b0874deae69c4c2d1dcaba1aa4c44f21b75b82051acdf7ea383df95f7f3cec" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived from the original matrix A=[[1,3],[3,1]]: (1,1) is an eigenvector with eigenvalue 4 and (-1,1) is an eigenvector with eigenvalue -2. Dividing each by sqrt(2) gives orthonormal columns of the displayed P, so P^T P=I and P^T AP=diag(4,-2). This proves original option c (index 2), matching the authentic worked solution. The history screenshot identifies submitted option b, whose first diagonal entry is 7, as the Incorrect answer. The stored bmatrix and captured bracketed aligned notation contain identical D and P matrices; preserve the text field type." "bindings" {"problem" "d6d6d2b12dc3e2c5c0232c9e6ece79528ba998dac5c7e97ce381f3f5f69b3e57" "worked_solution" "caadfdf6f7d15d3afbf164be75fee3bfe548c935ecac94038e94d778b2d749b4" "original_field" "845b79c6c5508af6ecfe10a1766ee3813695415b034f1fbb7a485cb8148a72e6" "reviewed_field" "ec9b45eb2102f200b4137a011bc2df8667ec54f945c60fa0d20455705bf3debe" "raw_widget" "e0a951d74f042356db2dcda5ce192b0078ae1b12ca0c60decf5ad2ac3446d4df"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-94056-before.png" "sha256" "98188ccd2b04c9048579b7aecf19bb42dfbb13df2c150634fb464d09d7ec63a8"} {"path" "q-94056-before.json" "sha256" "2df35a93d82493f007e8a5de9f7c2a991b9efda1dcb180c7c329625065b8e1eb"} {"path" "history-q-